Yes — for your Dyneema-based home rig, use a single-layer, helically grooved spool, a locked eye splice at the moving carriage, and a clamped drum dead-end at the motor side. Helical grooves give the rope a defined path and reduce scrubbing, and Dyneema winch lines are generally best wound under the drum rather than over it.

Spool design
A helical groove keeps the line in place during loading, and under-wound spooling reduces side-load and flange wear. For your first build with 0.8 mm to 1.0 mm Dyneema and about 4 m usable line per corner, I recommend this exact spool: barrel diameter 22 mm, barrel width 60 mm, flange diameter 40 mm, groove pitch 1.1 mm, groove depth 0.5 mm, center bore to match your coupler, and one 2 mm cross-hole 6 mm in from one flange for the line tail.

