Splice and attachment
For Dyneema, the best end termination is an eye splice, and a practical method is to lock it with seizing so it does not creep when unloaded. Eye-spliced loops are also commonly preferred over repeatedly shortening line with knots.

My exact recommendation is:

Carriage end: 10 mm stainless thimble + locked eye splice.

Bury length: at least 50x line diameter for hobby use.

Locking: whipping twine seizing over the bury exit, then a tiny drop of flexible adhesive only on the seizing, not soaked into the whole line.

Drum end: pass the line through the 2 mm cross-hole, wrap half a turn around the barrel, and clamp it with a small M3 washer plate or grub-screw pad.

For a temporary prototype, a doubled uni knot is an easy braid-to-braid option, but for the final carriage attachment, use the eye splice instead.

Tensioning method
Winch line should be spooled onto the drum under load, slowly, while checking the wraps as you go. That matters even more with Dyneema because loose wraps can cross, slip, or dig unevenly into the drum path.

Use this exact method:

Put the carriage at the center of the frame.

Add a temporary ballast so total suspended mass is about 20% more than your real camera carriage.

Free-spool all four lines and remove slack by hand.

Wind each drum slowly until each line is just taut.

Use a small hanging scale inline and set initial preload to about 10 N to 15 N per line for a 300 g to 500 g carriage.

Run the carriage through a slow square path, return to center, and respool any drum that shows overlap.

For long-term consistency, the easiest home method is: equal line lengths at home position, slight downward ballast on the carriage, and software that never allows any line to go slack.

