Step-by-step Dyneema eye splice tutorial for Pi project cable attachment
Completed 2 steps
Dyneema eye splices are the best permanent attachment for your Spidercam carriage because they retain nearly full line strength, resist slipping under load, and fit neatly around a small thimble. For your Raspberry Pi project, use a simple buried tail eye splice with a thimble and locking seizing to prevent unload creep.

Tools needed
Dyneema fid or splicing wand (plastic or metal, sized for your line).

Sharp scissors.

Whipping twine or thin Dyneema thread.

Large sewing needle.

Electrical tape.

10 mm stainless steel thimble (matches 0.8–1.0 mm line).

Marker pen.

Step-by-step splice
Measure and mark: Cut line to length plus 1 m tail. Mark the throat point (where the splice starts) so the eye is about 8x line diameter (e.g., 8 mm for 1 mm line). Tape the ends to prevent fraying.

Prepare thimble: Slide thimble onto line. Form a loop around it and hold throat at your mark.

Separate strands: At throat mark, split braid into working end (tail) and standing part. Use fid to push into standing part about 2–3 cm from throat.

Taper tail: Pull out 2 strands at 1/4 distance from throat, then 2 more at equal intervals. Cut flush later. This makes a smooth bury.

Bury tail: Hook tapered tail to fid loop. Push fid through standing part toward throat. Pull tail through (milk the rope by sliding hands from throat outward to smooth). Bury to 30x diameter (e.g., 30 cm for 1 mm line).

Lock stitch: Pull single strand from scrap Dyneema, thread needle. Stitch tightly back from throat along bury, then perpendicular. This locks the tail.

Seizing: Wrap whipping twine around bury exit (clove hitch start), 20–30 turns, pass needle through core, finish with reef knot. Trim and dab flexible glue (e.g., silicone sealant).

Final check: Milk entire splice. Load test by hanging 2–3x carriage weight. Eye should not twist or pull out.

This method matches tutorials for single-braid Dyneema and works for your 0.8–1.0 mm line.

Project tips
Do one splice first on scrap line.

Thimble prevents abrasion at carriage eyelet.

Identical splices on all four lines for balance.

If line slips unloaded, add more seizing turns.

